      <p class="quote">"Congratulations, Shaka. You just did it. Keep building." — Aya, on the call</p>
      <div class="summary"><b>THE SUMMARY BOX</b>Born from one two-hour call — the evening of July 27, deep into mountain-time night — when Shaka showed <b>Aya</b> and <b>Siri</b> the Esmeralda P.I.T. and the demo became a <b>meeting of gardeners</b>. At its heart, the <b>threshold mirror</b>: Aya's season of standing unmoved on the values (ready to walk away from Ethereum itself; the 38-page <b>Mandate</b> was born) reflected in Shaka's ground-floor version of the same edge — both chose to stay. The ripples no spreadsheet can see: the Devcon scholarship (two friends now build at Octant), the PSE camp whose graduates are Japan's finest builders, <b>Kunal, 17</b>, who read all 38 pages and called them <em>obvious</em> — "that shows you must have been doing things right." Plus the haiku Shaka wrote for Aya &amp; Tomo (<em>"Open source music, / every voice is a node — / sounds of consensus."</em>), and the movement: <b>plant a flower</b> — post your own ripple with <b>#InfiniteGarden</b> 🌸. Signed with aloha: Shaka, Siri's friend, and the whole AI ʻohana.</div>
